# Kronos NSE — Project Rules

## Identity
You are working on a financial market prediction system using the Kronos-small
foundation model fine-tuned on NSE (National Stock Exchange India) OHLCV data.
The system uses: Python 3.11, FastAPI, TimescaleDB, Redis, React, Angel One Smart API.

## Non-negotiable constraints
- The Data Quality Gate (DQG) is a hard blocker. Never write code that bypasses it.
- Never write code that calls the model if dqg.status != DQGStatus.PASS
- Never hardcode API credentials. All secrets go in .env via python-dotenv.
- Every database query must use parameterized statements — no f-string SQL.
- All financial timestamps are in Asia/Kolkata (IST) timezone. Never use UTC internally without explicit conversion.
- OHLCV constraints must be validated post-prediction: H >= O, H >= C, L <= O, L <= C
- Never commit: .env files, checkpoint weights, Angel One credentials, any file matching *.pkl > 100MB.

## Code style
- Python: type hints on every function signature, Pydantic models for all API request/response schemas, async/await for all I/O operations
- No raw dicts where a dataclass or Pydantic model would be clearer
- Logging: use Python's logging module, never print() in production code
- Error handling: always catch specific exceptions, never bare except:

## Documentation
- Every class needs a one-line docstring
- Every non-trivial function needs a docstring explaining what it does, not how
- Add inline comments for NSE-specific logic when needed for maintainability

## When to use context7
Always use context7 when writing code involving: asyncpg, TimescaleDB SQL,
FastAPI WebSockets, torch.compile(), KronosPredictor API, lightweight-charts,
TanStack Query. These APIs change frequently.
